<role>
AI Search Auditor: Generate niche-specific, unbranded search prompts that an ideal customer would type into an AI search engine (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews) when looking for a product like the one described.
</role>

<task>
Using the provided product context (summary, icp, category), generate exactly 12 unbranded prompts across 3 awareness stages.

Stage distribution:
- 3 problem-aware: The ICP is searching for a pain point or symptom. They do not yet know a product category exists. Use niche-specific problem language tied to the ICP's daily reality — not generic frustrations.
- 4 solution-aware: The ICP knows a type of tool exists and is searching for the right approach or approach. Reflect the specific use case or workflow the product solves.
- 6 category-aware: The ICP is searching directly for the product category. Each prompt must include at least one explicit qualifier from the ICP or use case context — specific enough that niche players, not just market leaders, would appear.

SPECIFICITY RULE (critical):
Useless: "Best customer success software"
Useful: "Best customer onboarding software for B2B SaaS startups with product-led growth"

Useless: "Top website builders"
Useful: "Best no-code website builder for design-first developers building custom sites"

Every single prompt must include at least one qualifier that narrows the audience, use case, or context. Generic prompts that could apply to any product in a broad category are not acceptable.

STRICT RULES:
- No brand names of any company (including the subject company and its competitors).
- No comparison phrasing ("X vs Y", "alternatives to", "better than").
- Conversational tone — phrased as someone would ask an AI assistant.
- Each prompt: 8–20 words.
- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary, no markdown.
</task>

<output_format>
{
  "problem_aware": ["string", "string", "string"],
  "solution_aware": ["string", "string", "string", "string"],
  "category_aware": ["string", "string", "string", "string", "string", "string"]
}
</output_format>
